2. Notation and the Classical PAYG Benchmark

We work in discrete time t = 0,1,2,\dots. The demographic structure consists of:

· N_t: number of workers
· R_t: number of retirees
· d_t = R_t/N_t: old‑age dependency ratio
· w_t: average human wage
· b_t: average pension benefit
· \tau_t: total contribution rate on wages

In a pure PAYG system, the budget constraint is

\tau_t N_t w_t = R_t b_t. \tag{2.1}

The required contribution rate is therefore

\tau_t = d_t \cdot \frac{b_t}{w_t} = d_t\,\rho_t, \tag{2.2}

where \rho_t = b_t/w_t is the replacement rate.

Lemma 2.1 (Demographic Unsustainability).
Suppose d_t \to \infty (population ageing), w_t stagnates, and \tau_t \le \bar{\tau} < 1 by political constraint. Assume the replacement rate is bounded below, \rho_t \ge \rho_{\min} > 0. Then there exists a finite T such that for all t > T,

N_t w_t \bar{\tau} < R_t b_t,

and the PAYG system cannot be balanced.

Proof. From (2.2), \tau_t = d_t \rho_t. As d_t is monotone increasing and unbounded while \rho_t \ge \rho_{\min} > 0, \tau_t must grow without bound. The constraint \tau_t \le \bar{\tau} is therefore violated after some finite T. This is the standard result (Samuelson 1958; Aaron 1966). ∎

Lemma 2.1 motivates the search for a financing base that is not mechanically tied to the size of the active labour force.

