3. A CES Production Model with Artificial Intelligence

3.1 Three‑Factor CES Specification

Empirically, human labour and AI are not perfect substitutes. We therefore replace the linear sum Y = H + A + K with a nested CES production function. Let

Y_t = Z_t \Bigl( \alpha_H H_t^{\rho} + \alpha_A A_t^{\rho} + \alpha_K K_t^{\rho} \Bigr)^{1/\rho}, \tag{3.1}

where:

· H_t: human labour input (hours or efficiency units)
· A_t: AI productive input (capital stock of AI/robotics)
· K_t: traditional (non‑AI) automated capital
· Z_t: total factor productivity
· \alpha_H + \alpha_A + \alpha_K = 1
· \rho = \frac{\sigma - 1}{\sigma}, with \sigma > 0 the elasticity of substitution.

If \sigma = 1 (\rho = 0), (3.1) reduces to Cobb–Douglas, Y_t = Z_t H_t^{\alpha_H} A_t^{\alpha_A} K_t^{\alpha_K}. If \sigma > 1 (\rho > 0), the factors are gross substitutes; if \sigma < 1 (\rho < 0), they are gross complements.

3.2 Labour Share Collapse

Proposition 3.1 (Labour Share under AI Substitutability).
If \sigma > 1 and A_t/H_t \to \infty, then the labour share s_{H,t} = w_t H_t / Y_t tends to zero:

\lim_{t \to \infty} s_{H,t} = 0.

Proof. Under competitive factor markets, the labour share derived from (3.1) is

s_{H,t} = \frac{\alpha_H H_t^{\rho}}{\alpha_H H_t^{\rho} + \alpha_A A_t^{\rho} + \alpha_K K_t^{\rho}}. \tag{3.2}

Dividing numerator and denominator by H_t^{\rho} yields

s_{H,t} = \frac{\alpha_H}{\alpha_H + \alpha_A (A_t/H_t)^{\rho} + \alpha_K (K_t/H_t)^{\rho}}. \tag{3.3}

With \sigma > 1, \rho = (\sigma-1)/\sigma > 0. If A_t/H_t \to \infty, then (A_t/H_t)^{\rho} \to \infty, and the denominator diverges, driving s_{H,t} \to 0. ∎

Proposition 3.1 formalizes the erosion of the wage base when AI is a gross substitute for labour. Consequently, the ratio of total output to the wage bill, Y_t/(w_t H_t) = 1/s_{H,t}, grows without bound, opening the possibility of delinking pension financing from the number of workers.

