4. Pension Sustainability Index and AI Productivity Contribution

4.1 The Pension Sustainability Index (PSI)

Define annual pension obligations as O_t = R_t b_t. Let available sustainable resources be T_t. In its simplest form, T_t = \tau_t Y_t, where \tau_t is a levy on total output rather than on wages alone.

Definition 4.1 (PSI).

\text{PSI}_t \equiv \frac{T_t}{O_t}. \tag{4.1}

The system is sustainable if \text{PSI}_t \ge 1 for all future periods.

Theorem 4.1 (Decoupling from Demography).
Under the classical PAYG regime, \text{PSI}_t^{\text{class}} = \tau_t / (d_t \rho_t). Under the AI‑augmented regime with T_t = \tau_t Y_t,

\text{PSI}_t^{\text{AI}} = \tau_t \cdot \frac{Y_t}{R_t b_t}
      = \tau_t \cdot \frac{Y_t}{w_t N_t} \cdot \frac{w_t N_t}{R_t b_t}
      = \frac{\tau_t}{d_t \rho_t} \cdot \frac{1}{s_{H,t}}. \tag{4.2}

If s_{H,t} \to 0 as AI expands, \text{PSI}_t^{\text{AI}} can remain above unity even when d_t \to \infty, provided \tau_t is chosen appropriately and 1/s_{H,t} grows faster than d_t.

Proof. Immediate from the definitions. In the classical model, Y_t = w_t N_t (no other productive inputs), so s_{H,t}=1 and (4.2) reduces to the PAYG condition. With AI, the factor 1/s_{H,t} acts as a multiplier that can offset demographic pressure. ∎

Theorem 4.1 demonstrates the theoretical possibility of breaking the link between the number of workers and pension solvency.

4.2 The AI Productivity Contribution (APC)

We define the APC not as a tax rate but as a flow of resources attributable to AI‑generated value. Let Y_t^A = Y_t - Y_t|_{A=0} be the counterfactual output gain due to AI.

Definition 4.2 (APC flow).

\text{APC}_t = \theta_t \, Y_t^A, \qquad \theta_t \in [0,1],

where \theta_t is the social contribution coefficient.

The sustainability resource base becomes T_t = \tau_H w_t N_t + \theta_t Y_t^A + S_t, where S_t is any dividend from a sovereign AI wealth fund. For analytical tractability, we often work with the consolidated rate \tau_t on total output, with the APC subsumed within it.

Theorem 4.2 (Existence of Sustainable Contribution Rate).
Assume Y_t grows at rate g_Y and pension obligations O_t grow at rate g_R. If g_Y > g_R, there exists a \tau^* < 1 such that \text{PSI}_t = 1 for all t. In particular,

\tau^* = \frac{O_0}{Y_0} \lim_{t\to\infty} \left(\frac{1+g_R}{1+g_Y}\right)^t,

which is bounded and less than one if g_Y > g_R. If AI drives g_Y sufficiently high, \tau^* becomes arbitrarily small.

Proof. Set \text{PSI}_t = \tau_t Y_t / O_t = 1, hence \tau_t = O_t/Y_t. With constant growth rates, O_t = O_0 (1+g_R)^t and Y_t = Y_0 (1+g_Y)^t, giving \tau_t = (O_0/Y_0) \left(\frac{1+g_R}{1+g_Y}\right)^t. If g_Y > g_R, the ratio tends to zero, so \tau_t eventually falls below any constant. If g_Y \le g_R, the required rate diverges or fails to decline, making sustainability impossible without additional revenue sources. ∎

Theorem 4.2 identifies the fundamental growth condition: AI‑driven output must outpace the growth of pension liabilities. This is the primary empirical hypothesis of the framework.

