5. Calibration of \sigma, g_A, and g_R from Industry‑Level Data

To transform the theoretical conditions into testable hypotheses, we must calibrate the elasticity of substitution and the growth rates.

5.1 Elasticity of Substitution

We employ a nested CES structure separating non‑robot and robot/AI capital. Let \sigma_{H,\text{non‑robot }K} be the elasticity between human labour and non‑robot capital, and \sigma_{H,\text{robot }K} the elasticity between human labour and AI‑robotic capital. Structural estimates using IFR robot data and O*NET task data suggest:

\sigma_{H,\text{non‑robot }K} < 1 \quad \text{(complements)},\qquad
\sigma_{H,\text{robot }K} > 1 \quad \text{(substitutes)},

with the task‑level elasticity \zeta > 1 (8696118463702521493). For the aggregate CES (3.1), the relevant \sigma is the substitution between human labour and the AI factor. We calibrate \sigma via non‑linear least squares across 35 sectors (Mućk 2017, 5a9d), using:

· EU‑KLEMS data for wage bill wH,
· IFR data for robot/AI capital stock K^R,
· International Federation of Robotics price indices for the user cost r^R.

From the first‑order condition of a cost‑minimizing firm, the ratio of factor shares satisfies

\ln\left(\frac{w_t H_t}{r_t^A A_t}\right) = \text{constant} + \frac{\sigma-1}{\sigma} \ln\left(\frac{H_t}{A_t}\right) + \varepsilon_t,

identifying \sigma. Instrumenting with shift‑share exposure (Acemoglu & Restrepo) ensures identification against unobserved productivity shocks.

5.2 Growth Rates

The growth rate of AI‑augmented output, g_Y, is driven by AI capital deepening and TFP growth in the AI sector. The growth rate of pension obligations, g_R, is driven by demographic change and benefit indexation.

· g_R \approx n_R + g_b, where n_R is the growth rate of the number of retirees (from UN World Population Prospects) and g_b is the growth rate of average real benefits (determined by indexation rules: CPI or wage growth).
· g_A is the growth rate of the AI capital stock in efficiency units. We measure it via a 3‑year moving average of enterprise AI adoption rates from the Eurostat 2018‑2023 panel, combined with AI patenting data to proxy TFP growth in AI (g_{Z_A}).

Theorem 5.1 (Decoupling Condition – Empirical).
If \hat{\sigma} > 1 and \hat{g}_A > \hat{g}_R, then there exists a \bar{t} such that for all t > \bar{t}, \text{PSI}_t is strictly increasing in A_t even as N_t declines.

Proof. From (4.2), \text{PSI}_t = \frac{\tau_t}{R_t b_t} \cdot \frac{Y_t}{s_{H,t}}. The derivative with respect to A_t is

\frac{d \text{PSI}_t}{d A_t} = \frac{\tau_t}{R_t b_t} \left( \frac{\partial Y_t}{\partial A_t} \frac{1}{s_{H,t}} - Y_t \frac{1}{s_{H,t}^2} \frac{\partial s_{H,t}}{\partial A_t} \right).

For \sigma > 1, \partial s_{H,t}/\partial A_t < 0 (substitution effect), and \partial Y_t/\partial A_t > 0. As A_t grows, the numerator grows at rate g_A, while the denominator R_t b_t grows at rate g_R. If g_A > g_R, the ratio diverges and \text{PSI}_t increases. ∎

