6. Construction of Counterfactual Y_t^A via Task‑Based GE

A credible measurement of Y_t^A—the output attributable to AI—requires a structural model of task allocation. We follow the task‑based framework of Acemoglu, Kong, and Restrepo (2024, 4272431585651388688).

6.1 Model Setup

Final output is a CES aggregate over a continuum of tasks x \in [0,1]:

Y_t = \left( \int_0^1 y_t(x)^{\frac{\eta-1}{\eta}} dx \right)^{\frac{\eta}{\eta-1}}, \quad \eta > 1.

Each task can be produced by human labour or by AI capital, depending on whether the task has been automated. The set of automated tasks at time t is \mathcal{T}^A_t \subseteq [0,1].

· For x \notin \mathcal{T}^A_t, production uses only human labour: y_t(x) = \gamma_H(x) h_t(x), with unit cost c_H(x) = w_t / \gamma_H(x).
· For x \in \mathcal{T}^A_t, production uses AI capital: y_t(x) = \gamma_A(x) a_t(x), with unit cost c_A(x) = r_t^A / \gamma_A(x).

Automation occurs when c_A(x) \le c_H(x). The equilibrium labour share and wages are then determined by the assignment of tasks.

6.2 Counterfactual Construction

The counterfactual output without AI progress since a base year (e.g., 2006) is obtained by holding the set of automated tasks fixed at \mathcal{T}^A_0. Implementation steps:

1. Map O*NET tasks to ISCO occupations and then to 500 worker groups using US Census and EU‑LFS microdata (4272431585651388688).
2. Estimate task displacement via a propagation matrix:
   d\ln w_g = -\text{Propagation Matrix} \times \text{Automation Exposure}_g,
   where the propagation matrix captures GE ripple effects of task reallocation.
3. Build the counterfactual wage distribution by holding automation exposure at its 2006 level (8dec).
4. Compute Y_t^{CF} as the aggregate output under the counterfactual task assignment, and define
   Y_t^A = Y_t - Y_t^{CF}.

Theorem 6.1 (Decomposition of AI Value Added).
The AI‑attributable output can be decomposed as

Y_t^A = \int_{\mathcal{T}^A_t \setminus \mathcal{T}^A_0} \bigl[ c_H(x) - c_A(x) \bigr] \gamma_H(x) h_t(x) dx \;+\; \text{Productivity Effect},

where the first term is the direct cost saving from automation (small when micro‑elasticity is high), and the Productivity Effect captures the increase in TFP from the reallocation of labour to non‑automated tasks. The Productivity Effect dominates in advanced AI scenarios (4272431585651388688).

Proof. By the envelope theorem, the change in the cost function due to an expansion of the automated set equals the integral of cost differences, plus a residual representing the indirect efficiency gain from re‑optimization. ∎

