8. Multi‑Country Simulations with Heterogeneous AI Adoption

The impact of AI on pension systems is highly heterogeneous across countries. The US–Europe AI adoption gap is 95% explained by workforce composition and employer encouragement, not by technology (bc94).

8.1 Adoption Friction Model

For each country c, AI intensity is governed by an adoption friction \xi_{c,t}:

A_{c,t} = \xi_{c,t} \cdot A_t^{\text{frontier}},

\xi_{c,t} = f(\text{skills shortage}, \text{cost pressure}, \text{management practice}),

where the arguments are empirical determinants of AI adoption (01a3).

8.2 Calibration and Simulation Protocol

Using a panel of 28 European countries (2018–2023), we estimate \xi_c via fixed‑effects regression (7f51). Firm‑level surveys (2025–2026) provide updated determinants (525b). The simulation protocol is as follows:

1. Draw AI growth shocks g_{A,c} \sim \mathcal{N}(\bar{g}_A \xi_c, \sigma_c^2).
2. For eight representative European pension systems, simulate PSI paths using detailed micro‑simulation models with rational utility‑maximizing households facing country‑specific pension rules (89c7).
3. For staggered AI policy adoption, apply the Callaway–Sant’Anna difference‑in‑differences estimator to build cohort‑specific counterfactuals (3a5f).

Theorem 8.1 (Divergence).
If adoption frictions \xi_c are heterogeneous and countries maintain the same contribution parameter \theta, then the cross‑sectional variance of \text{PSI}_{c,t} grows without bound unless offsetting fiscal transfers are implemented.

Proof. From (4.2), \text{PSI}_{c,t} \propto \tau_{c,t} / (d_{c,t} \rho_{c,t}) \times 1/s_{H,c,t}. The labour share s_{H,c,t} collapses at different speeds across countries due to \xi_c. Even if \tau_t and demographic paths are identical, the denominator s_{H,c,t} diverges, causing \text{Var}(\text{PSI}_{c,t}) \to \infty as t \to \infty. ∎

This result calls for coordination mechanisms, possibly through an AI‑productivity solidarity fund.

