9. Political Economy Constraints on Rent Extraction

AI is increasingly a rentier asset: its returns derive from constructed scarcity and access control rather than commodity exchange (1184824267445702030). The ability to extract a social contribution \theta_t from AI rents is constrained by the mobility of AI capital and the concentration of infrastructure.

9.1 Definition of AI Rents

Define the rent component as

\pi_t^A = Y_t^A - \text{cost of AI capital at competitive return}.

Extraction is bounded by a Laffer curve: if \theta_t is set too high, AI investment I_t^A declines, reducing future Y_t^A.

Formal constraint:

\theta_t \le \theta^{\text{Laffer}}_t, \quad \text{where} \quad \theta^{\text{Laffer}}_t = \arg\max_{\theta} \theta \, Y_t^A(\theta).

The function Y_t^A(\theta) is decreasing in \theta due to reduced investment and potential offshoring of AI capital. The optimal harvesting problem of Section 7 already incorporates this trade‑off.

9.2 Mechanisms of Rent Enclosure

Rents are enclosed via:

· Data enclosure: proprietary training data and network effects,
· API pricing and ecosystem lock‑in: productivity gains appropriated upward through platform rents (1184824267445702030),
· Monopoly overcharging: leveraging market power to undercut competition (88ae).

These mechanisms determine the politically feasible upper bound \bar{\theta}_t. A highly concentrated AI infrastructure layer (cloud compute, foundation models) allows a higher extraction rate, while international tax competition (race to the bottom) pushes \bar{\theta}_t downward.

Theorem 9.1 (Political Sustainability of APC).
An APC is politically feasible only if \theta_t \le \theta^{\text{Laffer}}_t and if the revenue is visibly linked to social protection. Otherwise, capital flight erodes the base.

