10. Legal Frameworks for AI‑Generated Value Added

Enforcement of any APC requires a legally operational definition of AI‑generated value added that avoids overclaiming (fbb1).

10.1 Proposed Legal Taxonomy

We propose three legally distinct instruments:

1. Corporate surcharge or rent tax: Targets the excess returns from AI, identified as the difference between actual profit and the competitive return on AI capital.
2. Consumption tax: Levied on the purchase or use of AI services, easy to administer but regressive.
3. Excise tax / user fee: Applied to specific inputs like compute (tokens) or data, akin to resource taxation.

10.2 Legal Definition of Y^A for Tax Purposes

Define the AI‑attributable value added for a firm as

Y^A = \text{Revenue} - \text{Cost of non‑AI inputs} - \text{Normal return on non‑AI capital} - \text{Imputed labour cost of human workers},

where the ratio of AI to human task execution is identified via audited task logs. The definition mirrors transfer‑pricing rules for intangibles.

Enforceability conditions:

· No claim without license: Validation of AI‑attributable output does not determine the claim level (0ddd); a separate legal instrument sets \theta.
· Technical prerequisites: Mandatory model cards and compute‑accounting standards for a compute tax.
· Rent tax requirements: Mandatory separation of AI capital assets in financial statements.

These conditions ensure that Y_t^A is separable and auditable, satisfying Condition C2 of our framework.

