11. Conclusion and Research Agenda

This dissertation has established a mathematical foundation for an AI‑augmented pension system. We have shown that when AI is a gross substitute for human labour, the labour share collapses, making the traditional wage‑based tax base unsustainable. A shift to a productivity‑based financing system is theoretically possible if AI‑driven output growth exceeds pension liability growth. We have calibrated the key parameters, constructed a task‑based counterfactual for AI value added, derived an optimal harvesting rule under uncertainty, modeled international heterogeneity, analysed political‑economy constraints, and proposed a legal taxonomy for enforcement.

The framework identifies three necessary empirical conditions without which the system collapses back to demographic unsustainability:

· C1: \hat{\sigma} > 1 and \hat{g}_A > \hat{g}_R;
· C2: Y_t^A is separable and auditable;
· C3: For fund‑based systems, the return on the sovereign AI fund exceeds the growth rate of pension obligations, r^S > g_R.

Future work should implement the full stochastic optimal control with deep learning (Section 7), run the multi‑country simulations with the proposed micro‑simulation models (Section 8), and develop the international legal instruments for AI value‑added taxation (Section 10). The transition dynamics and intergenerational fairness constraints also deserve dedicated modelling.

The central lesson is clear: in an age when productive capacity is increasingly decoupled from human labour, the social contract of retirement must evolve from a labour‑based contribution model to a productivity‑based one. Whether this transition is politically chosen or imposed by crisis remains the great open question.

