Appendix A. Proofs of Main Theorems

A.1 Lemma 2.1 (Demographic Unsustainability)

Statement. Under PAYG with d_t\to\infty, \rho_t\ge\rho_{\min}>0, and \tau_t\le\bar{\tau}<1, there exists T such that for all t>T, N_t w_t\bar{\tau}<R_t b_t (system cannot balance).

Proof. The PAYG budget constraint (2.1) implies \tau_t = d_t\rho_t. Since d_t is monotone increasing and unbounded, and \rho_t\ge\rho_{\min}>0, \tau_t diverges. The inequality \tau_t\le\bar{\tau} fails for all t exceeding some finite T. Budget balance requires N_t w_t \tau_t = R_t b_t; substituting the failed inequality gives N_t w_t\bar{\tau} \ge N_t w_t\tau_t = R_t b_t only if \tau_t\le\bar{\tau} holds, contradicting divergence. Hence N_t w_t\bar{\tau} < R_t b_t. ∎

A.2 Proposition 3.1 (Labour Share Collapse)

Statement. In the CES model (3.1) with \sigma>1 and A_t/H_t\to\infty, the labour share s_{H,t}\to 0.

Proof. From (3.2),

s_{H,t} = \frac{\alpha_H H_t^\rho}{\alpha_H H_t^\rho + \alpha_A A_t^\rho + \alpha_K K_t^\rho}.

Dividing numerator and denominator by H_t^\rho,

s_{H,t} = \frac{\alpha_H}{\alpha_H + \alpha_A (A_t/H_t)^\rho + \alpha_K (K_t/H_t)^\rho}.

If \sigma>1 then \rho = (\sigma-1)/\sigma > 0. As A_t/H_t\to\infty, (A_t/H_t)^\rho\to\infty; since K_t/H_t is non-negative, the denominator diverges. Hence s_{H,t}\to 0. ∎

A.3 Theorem 4.1 (Decoupling from Demography)

Proof. From Definition 4.1 and T_t=\tau_t Y_t,

\text{PSI}_t^{\text{AI}} = \frac{\tau_t Y_t}{R_t b_t}.

Write R_t b_t = d_t N_t b_t = d_t \rho_t N_t w_t. Also Y_t = (Y_t/(N_t w_t)) N_t w_t = (1/s_{H,t}) N_t w_t. Hence

\text{PSI}_t^{\text{AI}} = \tau_t \frac{(1/s_{H,t}) N_t w_t}{d_t \rho_t N_t w_t} = \frac{\tau_t}{d_t \rho_t}\cdot \frac{1}{s_{H,t}}.

In the classical case s_{H,t}=1, recovering the PAYG expression. If s_{H,t}\to 0 as AI expands, the factor 1/s_{H,t} can offset a rising d_t. ∎

A.4 Theorem 4.2 (Existence of Sustainable Contribution Rate)

Proof. With constant growth rates, Y_t = Y_0(1+g_Y)^t and O_t = O_0(1+g_R)^t. Setting \text{PSI}_t = \tau_t Y_t/O_t = 1 gives \tau_t = (O_0/Y_0)((1+g_R)/(1+g_Y))^t. If g_Y > g_R, the ratio tends to zero, so \tau_t eventually lies below any constant \tau^*; one can set \tau^* as the supremum of the required rates. If g_Y \le g_R, the ratio does not tend to zero and sustainability without additional revenue is impossible. ∎

A.5 Theorem 5.1 (Empirical Decoupling Condition)

Proof. For \sigma>1, \partial s_{H,t}/\partial A_t < 0 and \partial Y_t/\partial A_t > 0. The derivative

\frac{d\text{PSI}_t}{dA_t} = \frac{\tau_t}{R_t b_t} \left[ \frac{\partial Y_t}{\partial A_t}\frac{1}{s_{H,t}} - \frac{Y_t}{s_{H,t}^2}\frac{\partial s_{H,t}}{\partial A_t} \right] > 0.

The growth rate of the numerator term is g_A, while the denominator grows at g_R. Thus if g_A > g_R, PSI eventually rises even if N_t declines. ∎

A.6 Theorem 6.1 (Decomposition of AI Value Added)

Proof. By the envelope theorem, the change in the minimized cost function when a small measure of tasks \Delta\mathcal{T} is automated is

\Delta C = \int_{\Delta\mathcal{T}} [c_A(x) - c_H(x)] y_t(x) dx + o(\Delta\mathcal{T}).

The total change in output due to automation is the sum of direct cost savings (first term) and the productivity effect from re-allocating labour to remaining tasks, which equals the increase in TFP in the CES aggregator. ∎

A.7 Theorem 7.1 (Optimal APC Rule under CRRA)

We present the full stochastic control derivation.

State variables: X_t = (Y_t^A, S_t). The dynamics:

dY_t^A = \mu_A Y_t^A dt + \sigma_A Y_t^A dW_t + J_t dN_t,

dS_t = [(r^S - \phi) S_t + \theta_t Y_t^A - D_t] dt,

with D_t = \phi S_t (dividend rule) and the sustainability constraint \theta_t Y_t^A + D_t \ge O_t (assumed binding in optimum). The planner chooses \theta_t to maximize \mathbb{E}\int_0^\infty e^{-\beta t} \frac{c_t^{1-\gamma}}{1-\gamma} dt, where c_t = Y_t^A + (r^S - \phi) S_t - \theta_t Y_t^A (resources left after contribution). For simplicity, set O_t absorbable via \theta, so \theta_t Y_t^A = O_t in equilibrium (binding). The optimisation is over the trajectory of \theta_t around that constraint.

The Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman (HJB) equation for the value function V(Y,S):

\beta V = \max_{\theta} \Big\{ \frac{c^{1-\gamma}}{1-\gamma} + \mu_A Y V_Y + \frac{1}{2}\sigma_A^2 Y^2 V_{YY} + \lambda \big[ V(Y+J, S) - V(Y,S) \big] + V_S[ (r^S-\phi)S + \theta Y - \phi S ] \Big\},

with c = Y + (r^S-\phi)S - \theta Y. First-order condition w.r.t. \theta:

c^{-\gamma} (-Y) + V_S Y = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; V_S = c^{-\gamma}.

Thus the marginal value of the sovereign fund equals the marginal utility of consumption. Envelope condition w.r.t. S:

\beta V_S = c^{-\gamma} (r^S-\phi) + V_{SS}[(r^S-\phi)S + \theta Y - \phi S] + \mu_A Y V_{SY} + \frac{1}{2}\sigma_A^2 Y^2 V_{SYY} + \lambda [V_S(Y+J, S) - V_S(Y,S)].

Using V_S = c^{-\gamma}, one obtains a Euler-type condition linking the expected return on the fund. In equilibrium, \theta Y = O, which pins down the required harvest. Under CRRA and log-linear approximations, the optimal \theta_t is procyclical with Y_t^A and countercyclical with S_t, as claimed. The precautionary term arises from the third derivative of utility via the variance term. For a rigorous derivation via the stochastic maximum principle, see the FBSDE approach in Appendix D.

A.8 Theorem 8.1 (Cross-Country Divergence)

Proof. From (4.2), \text{PSI}_{c,t} \propto 1/s_{H,c,t}. If AI adoption is heterogeneous (\xi_c), the labour shares s_{H,c,t} diverge over time due to different speeds of collapse. Even if \tau_t and demographic paths are identical, the variance of 1/s_{H,c,t} grows without bound as t\to\infty unless some countries reach the same limiting share zero; but with heterogeneous \xi_c, the rates of convergence differ, producing arbitrarily large cross-sectional dispersion. ∎

A.9 Theorem 9.1 (Political Feasibility of APC)

Proof. The Laffer condition states that for \theta > \theta^{\text{Laffer}}, the present value of AI rents decreases. Therefore, any legislated \theta exceeding the revenue-maximising rate reduces the sustainable resource base. Political feasibility requires that the rate be below both the revenue-maximising rate and the threshold at which capital flight erodes the base below that required for PSI=1. ∎

