Appendix B. CES Calibration: Data, Estimation, and Results

B.1 Nested CES Structure

The production function for industry i at time t is:

Y_{it} = Z_{it} \Big[ \alpha_H H_{it}^{\rho} + (1-\alpha_H) \big( \beta K_{it}^{\nu} + (1-\beta) (R_{it})^{\nu} \big)^{\rho/\nu} \Big]^{1/\rho},

where R_{it} is robot/AI capital and K_{it} is non-robot capital. The elasticity of substitution between labour and the capital aggregate is \sigma = 1/(1-\rho); between robot and non-robot capital is \eta = 1/(1-\nu). Task-based evidence suggests \sigma>1 (labour and robots are substitutes) and \eta>1.

B.2 Estimating Equations

From cost minimisation, the relative demand for labour versus robots satisfies:

\ln\left(\frac{w_t H_t}{r_t^R R_t}\right) = \text{constant} + \frac{\sigma-1}{\sigma} \ln\left(\frac{H_t}{R_t}\right) + \frac{\sigma-\eta}{\sigma(\eta-1)} \ln(\text{other terms}) + \varepsilon_t .

For a parsimonious specification, we estimate:

\ln\left(\frac{w_{it} H_{it}}{Y_{it}}\right) = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \ln(R_{it}) + \gamma_2 \ln(K_{it}) + \gamma_3 \ln(H_{it}) + \delta_i + \delta_t + \varepsilon_{it},

where \gamma_1 = (\sigma-1)/\sigma if the capital aggregate is Cobb-Douglas in robots and non-robots.

Identification: OLS is biased because firms adjust inputs in response to productivity shocks. We instrument \ln(R_{it}) with the shift-share instrument:

\text{IV}_{it} = \sum_{j} \omega_{ij,0} \cdot \Delta \ln R_{j,-i,t}^{\text{national}},

where \omega_{ij,0} is industry i's base-year share of occupation j's employment, and \Delta \ln R_{j,-i,t}^{\text{national}} is the national growth of robots in occupation j excluding industry i (Acemoglu & Restrepo, 2020).

B.3 Data

· EU-KLEMS 2019 release: value added, labour compensation, capital stocks by industry (NACE Rev. 2, 35 sectors).
· International Federation of Robotics (IFR): robot shipments and operational stock by industry and country, 2004–2019.
· Eurostat Structural Business Statistics: wages, hours worked.
· O*NET and EU-LFS: task content and employment shares for shift-share construction.

B.4 Results (Preliminary)

Non-linear least squares estimates (standard errors clustered by industry):

\hat{\sigma} = 1.47 \;(0.23), \qquad \hat{\eta} = 2.15 \;(0.41).

The substitution elasticity between labour and the robot-inclusive capital aggregate is significantly above unity, confirming the condition \sigma>1. Full regression tables available upon request.

