Appendix C. Task-Based GE Model and Counterfactual Construction

C.1 Model Setup

Following Acemoglu, Kong, and Restrepo (2024, WP 4272431585651388688), final output Y is a CES aggregate over tasks x\in[0,1] with elasticity \eta.

Y = \left( \int_0^1 y(x)^{\frac{\eta-1}{\eta}} dx \right)^{\frac{\eta}{\eta-1}} .

Task x can be produced by labour with productivity \gamma_H(x) or by AI capital with productivity \gamma_A(x) if x \in \mathcal{T}^A. Unit costs are c_H(x)=w/\gamma_H(x) and c_A(x)=r^A/\gamma_A(x). The equilibrium allocation assigns tasks to the cheaper factor; the marginal automated task x^* satisfies c_H(x^*) = c_A(x^*).

C.2 Counterfactual Algorithm

1. Baseline equilibrium (year 0): Solve for w_0, r^A_0, the set \mathcal{T}^A_0, and the task assignment using actual data.
2. Counterfactual scenario: Freeze the set of automated tasks at \mathcal{T}^A_0 while allowing total factor supplies H and R to grow as observed.
3. Re-solve for counterfactual wages w_t^{CF} and output Y_t^{CF}.
4. The AI-attributable output is Y_t^A = Y_t - Y_t^{CF}.

The propagation matrix \mathbf{P} linking task automation to occupation-level wage changes is derived from a first-order approximation:

d\ln w_g = -\sum_{x} \mathbf{P}_{g,x} \cdot \Delta \mathbf{1}_{x\in\mathcal{T}^A_t},

where \mathbf{P}_{g,x} captures the general equilibrium reallocation of labour across tasks and occupations. It is estimated using the Leontief inverse of the task–occupation employment matrix.

Data inputs: O*NET task importance scores mapped to ISCO occupations; US Census and EU-LFS microdata for 500 worker groups.

