Appendix E. Multi-Country Simulation Protocol

E.1 Data and Calibration of Adoption Frictions

We estimate AI adoption intensity \xi_{c,t} from:

\log(\text{AI}_{c,t}) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{skills\_shortage}_{c} + \beta_2 \text{cost\_pressure}_{c} + \beta_3 \text{management\_quality}_{c} + \gamma_c + \gamma_t + \varepsilon_{c,t},

using the Eurostat 2018–2023 enterprise survey panel (7f51). Country fixed effects \gamma_c capture persistent frictions. The fitted values give \xi_{c,t}.

E.2 Pension Microsimulation

For eight European countries (DE, FR, IT, ES, SE, PL, NL, DK), we use dynamic microsimulation models with rational expectations (89c7). Each model simulates a representative sample of 50,000 individuals, incorporating:

· Detailed pension rules (points, notional accounts, defined benefit)
· Labour supply responses to policy changes
· Stochastic mortality and fertility aligned with UN WPP 2022.

AI shocks enter through wage growth differentials by skill group, derived from the task exposure and propagation matrix.

E.3 Difference-in-Differences for Policy Evaluation

Staggered AI pension reforms are evaluated with the Callaway–Sant’Anna estimator (3a5f), using cohort-specific average treatment effects on the PSI. The treatment variable is the introduction of an APC in a given year. The control group consists of countries that have not yet adopted the reform. Covariates include pre-reform AI adoption and demographic trends.

