Appendix F. Legal and Institutional Design Details

F.1 Legal Definition of AI-Attributable Value Added

For a given enterprise, the AI-generated surplus is defined as:

Y^A = \underbrace{\text{Revenue} - \text{Cost of intermediate inputs} - \text{Capital depreciation (non-AI)} - \text{Imputed labour cost} - \text{Normal return on tangible capital}}_{\text{Conventional residual}} - \underbrace{\text{Licensing fees for third-party AI}}_{ \text{deductible} }.

This residual is then partitioned into a “competitive” return on AI-specific intangible capital (determined by comparable uncontrolled price method) and an “AI rent” which is subject to the APC.

F.2 Enforcement Mechanisms

· Model cards and compute logs: AI developers must disclose the computational resources consumed and the task domains automated, enabling verifiable attribution.
· Segmented financial reporting: Firms above a threshold must report AI capital as a separate asset class, with associated revenues.
· International coordination: A “Global AI Tax Compact” to prevent profit shifting, modelled on the OECD BEPS 2.0 framework, sets a minimum effective APC rate for AI rents.

F.3 Illustrative Legislative Provisions

· AI Rent Surcharge: A surtax on corporate profits exceeding a fixed return on AI assets, with the rate \theta set annually by an independent fiscal council referencing the estimated PSI gap.
· Compute Excise: A fixed levy per petaflop-hour of training compute, collected at the cloud provider level and earmarked for a sovereign AI pension fund.

